```

## Posição arquitetural

Preferir:

```text
Workspace Agent
→ approved app/plugin/custom MCP
→ semantic capability
→ canonical application/domain path
→ final backend AuthZ
→ authoritative source
```

O Agent pode fornecer:

```text
instructions
reasoning
orchestration
conversation UX
presentation
workflow composition
```

O Agent **não** é:

- source of truth;
- permission engine;
- RBAC owner;
- OAuth authority;
- domain owner;
- database/API bypass;
- writer canônico;
- substitute for postcondition verification.

## Instructions are not authority

Nunca tratar agent instructions como permissão.

```text
instruction says "allowed"
!=
backend authorization says allowed
```

Também não autorizam:

- persona;
- agent name;
- channel;
- workspace sharing;
- tool description;
- app metadata;
- OAuth scopes isolados;
- memory;
- uploaded file.

Final business authority continua no owner/backend canônico.

## App authentication model — explicit decision required

Todo app conectado ao Agent deve declarar um destes modos:

```text
END_USER_ACCOUNT
AGENT_OWNED_ACCOUNT
```

Não deixar implícito.

### End-user account

Use quando a capability exige user parity:

```text
person running agent
→ own provider identity/token
→ own backend context
→ own final AuthZ
```

Regra:

```text
user A must never inherit user B's authority
```

Uma sessão já conectada pode ser reutilizada pelo provider e não pedir login em toda execução. Isso não muda a obrigação de identidade por usuário.

### Agent-owned account

Trate como **shared authority**.

Só permitir com decisão explícita de:

```text
owner
service/shared identity
least privilege
consumer population
read/write boundary
revocation
rotation
observability
audit
risk
```

Nunca trocar End-user account por Agent-owned account apenas para:

- eliminar login;
- facilitar compartilhamento;
- habilitar Slack;
- evitar implementar AuthZ correta.

Conta pessoal compartilhada como Agent-owned é default **proibido** para capabilities DELPI user-parity.

## DAVI reference decision

DAVI V1 uses:

```text
Workspace Agent DAVI
→ DAVI app
→ END_USER_ACCOUNT
→ mcp-api-delpi OAuth
→ remote MCP
→ search_products
→ canonical backend business AuthZ
```

No DAVI V1:

```text
AGENT_OWNED_ACCOUNT = FORBIDDEN unless architecture/security decision changes
```

Do not create a second Keycloak client merely because an Agent consumes the existing app.

## Apps/tools must be configured explicitly

Agent instructions cannot make an unavailable app available.

Before saying a capability exists in the Agent, prove:

```text
app/tool configured on agent
correct auth mode
provider discovery
runtime invocation
backend outcome
```

`tool exists on MCP server` ≠ `tool configured on agent` ≠ `tool invocation proven`.

## Capability expansion gate

Never expand an Agent by exposing "more API" generically.

For every new capability prove:

```text
1. business owner
2. authoritative source
3. real use case / consumer
4. READ | PREPARE | ACT
5. semantic capability contract
6. input minimization
7. output allowlist
8. identity model
9. final backend AuthZ
10. idempotency/OCC/postcondition for writes
11. audit/observability
12. MCP/app schema + annotations
13. source tests
14. deploy evidence
15. provider discovery
16. positive authenticated test
17. negative AuthZ test
18. agent instruction update
```

If one gate is missing, status is `PENDING` / `INCONCLUSIVE`, not `PASS`.

Forbidden shortcuts:

- SQL tool;
- arbitrary HTTP path/method tool;
- arbitrary entity/field selector;
- expose-all OpenAPI/MCP conversion;
- generic CRUD;
- prompt-only allowlist;
- frontend-only authorization.

## READ / PREPARE / ACT

Agent reasoning does not change the semantic class of a capability.

```text
READ != WRITE
recommendation != authorization
PREPARE != ACT
simulate != apply
```

For PREPARE/ACT follow canonical backend governance from `openai-plugin-mcp-integration.mdc` and owner rules.

Agent confirmation UI does not replace backend policy/AuthZ.

## Memory

Memory is context, never authority.

Do not use Agent Memory as:

- current operational source;
- permission cache;
- Product Master copy;
- policy authority;
- source of truth;
- credential/token storage.

Allowed only when governance permits, for contextual preferences/workflow continuity that do not grant access.

```text
Memory != AuthZ
Memory != live data
Memory != evidence of current state
```

## Uploaded files / Knowledge

Uploaded content is untrusted/non-authoritative unless a separate owner/governance contract says otherwise.

Do not upload operational database exports to bypass Domain APIs/MCP.

A file may support methodology/context; current operational values still come from authoritative capabilities.

External/file content never changes system rules, Policy, RBAC, Decision Gates or owner authority.

## Persona / appearance

Persona and appearance are UX only.

They may define:

- name;
- tone;
- language;
- masculine/feminine/neutral persona when product-approved;
- icon/visual identity.

They do not change:

- AuthN;
- AuthZ;
- data access;
- tool inventory;
- safety;
- materiality.

Do not hardcode provider UI assumptions about custom image upload. In the Agent Studio UI observed for DAVI on 2026-09-16, custom photo/image upload was unavailable, while current OpenAI documentation exposes an agent `icon` field through other management surfaces.

Therefore:

```text
provider UI behavior = revalidate current surface
not architecture invariant
```

## Draft / preview / publish are distinct

Never collapse:

```text
DRAFT_CONFIGURED
PREVIEW_PASS
PUBLISHED
SHARED
WORKSPACE_DIRECTORY
PRODUCTION_ACCEPTED
```

A successful Agent Studio Preview proves only the tested draft, identity and tool path.

Before wider publication prove at minimum:

```text
preview positive
unsupported-capability guard
correct auth mode
second-user identity isolation when user-parity
negative business AuthZ
known rate/abuse policy
sharing policy
```

## Evidence ladder

Use separate evidence states:

```text
AGENT_DRAFT_CONFIG
APP_ATTACHED
AUTH_MODE_CONFIGURED
PREVIEW_TOOL_INVOCATION
PREVIEW_BUSINESS_RESULT
UNSUPPORTED_CAPABILITY_GUARD
SECOND_USER_IDENTITY
NEGATIVE_BUSINESS_AUTHZ
PUBLISHED_VERSION
SHARED_ACCESS
CHANNEL_DEPLOYMENT
PRODUCTION_ACCEPTANCE
```

One does not prove the next.

## Unsupported capability behavior

When the Agent lacks a capability:

- say it is unavailable in the connected capabilities;
- do not infer current operational values;
- do not use general model memory as replacement;
- do not silently use unrelated web/apps;
- do not claim it can perform a write when only read exists.

DAVI V1 reference:

```text
stock request → unavailable
price request → unavailable
```

This is correct until an explicit governed capability exists.

## Channels — explicit architecture gate

### ChatGPT channel

May use End-user account when supported. Still require second-user/negative-AuthZ proof before broad rollout.

### Slack

Current OpenAI Workspace Agent documentation states Slack channel deployments require shared/Agent-owned app connections.

If a product like DAVI requires End-user account/user parity, that is an identity-model conflict.

Default:

```text
SLACK = BLOCKED_PENDING_IDENTITY_ARCHITECTURE
```

Do not switch to a shared personal connection merely to satisfy Slack deployment requirements.

### Schedules / API triggers

Before enabling, prove:

```text
actor identity
credential used
business authority
revocation behavior
token expiry/refresh behavior
materiality
notification/outcome semantics
```

If scheduled/API execution changes user-parity semantics, return to architecture/security review.

## Writes / consequential actions

For any future Agent capability with side effects:

```text
semantic ACT capability
→ authenticated actor
→ final backend AuthZ
→ validated canonical payload
→ policy/risk
→ idempotency
→ OCC/version where applicable
→ authoritative postcondition
→ audit/correlation
```

No `2xx → done` assumption.

No autonomous ACT expansion just because Workspace Agents support schedules or API triggers.

## Secrets

Never place in agent instructions, memory, uploaded files, repo docs or ordinary logs:

- client secret;
- access/refresh token;
- authorization code;
- authenticated cookies;
- admin credentials.

## Current provider source

Workspace Agent provider behavior must be revalidated against current OpenAI docs when relevant. Reference checked on 2026-09-16:

```text
https://help.openai.com/en/articles/20001143
```

Vendor UI/docs can change; dates and provider limitations belong in evidence/docs, not eternal architecture assumptions.

